# NLSY79: Do academic characteristics alter SES/race effects on BA-track college attendance?

**Outcome limitation (stated up front):** "BA degree completion" is not observed in nls_raw.csv.
Outcome used = BA-track college attendance proxy: ever lived in DORM/FRATERNITY/SORORITY quarters
(residence type 3), any wave 1981-1996. Results speak to college-track attendance, NOT verified
degree completion.

## Descriptives (complete-case n=5,095; outcome prevalence 16.2%)
- Dorm attendance by SES tercile: low 8.2% | mid 13.3% | high 27.1%
- By race (raw, full file): non-Black/non-Hispanic 17.8% | Black 16.3% | Hispanic 8.5%
- Correlations: ASVAB-class rank pct r=0.57; ASVAB-SES r=0.44; Hispanic-ASVAB r=-0.43 (likely
  Spanish-language ASVAB version effect); HGC79-dorm r=-0.13 (timing artifact: dorms observed
  mainly in 1981, when higher-HGC respondents had already left school)

## Nested models, logit (n=5,095) — M0: race+SES; M1: +academics
| term | beta M0 | beta M1 | % change | OR M0 -> M1 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| SES composite | 0.828 | 0.513 | -38% | 2.29 -> 1.67 |
| Black | -0.358 | -0.151 | -58% | 0.70 -> 0.86 |
| Hispanic | +0.311 | +1.052 | grows | 1.37 -> 2.86 |
| ASVAB composite (1 SD) | — | 0.745 (p<.001) | | OR 2.11 |
| Class-rank percentile | — | 1.460 (p<.001) | | OR 4.31 |
| HGC 1979 | — | 0.069 (p=0.26) | | — |
Pseudo-R2: 0.113 -> 0.203. M2 interactions: ses:ASVAB -0.014 (p=0.83); Black:ASVAB -0.362 (p=0.08);
Hisp:ASVAB -0.162 (p=0.26).

## Linear probability models (same sample) — consistent
SES: 0.090 -> 0.053 (-41%); Black: -0.012 -> +0.017 (sign flip; full attenuation); Hispanic:
0.030 -> 0.115 (+281%). M2: ses:ASVAB +0.041 (p<.001); Black:ASVAB -0.046 (p=0.010).

## Sensitivity runs
- Adding log family net wealth 1985 (n=4,565): SES attenuation 37%, Black 50%, Hispanic grows — same pattern.
- Missing-indicator imputation, full N=12,686 (outcome 9.4%): SES attenuation 43%, Black 83%,
  Hispanic grows — same pattern.

## Robust conclusions
1. Adjusting for academic characteristics (ASVAB achievement, class-rank percentile, baseline
   schooling) roughly halves the SES gradient (OR 2.3 -> 1.7; LPM 9.0 -> 5.3 pct points per SD).
2. The raw Black disadvantage in BA-track attendance disappears (and slightly reverses) once
   academic achievement and class rank are controlled — academic characteristics account for the
   Black gap in this proxy.
3. Hispanic respondents' lower raw attendance is fully explained by much lower measured ASVAB
   scores; net of achievement they show the HIGHEST attendance — consistent with measurement bias
   in the ASVAB for Spanish-language test takers rather than lower college-track propensity.
4. Academic characteristics themselves are the strongest predictors: +1 SD ASVAB OR~2.1; top-of-class
   rank OR~4.3. There is some evidence (LPM M2) that SES amplifies and Black status attenuates the
   payoff to achievement.
